WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.188
A double free occurs when the same memory
allocation or resource is cleaned up more

2
00:00:04.268 --> 00:00:04.749
than once.

3
00:00:04.829 --> 00:00:08.570
In manual-memory environments, this class
of bug can cause severe reliability and

4
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.392
security problems because two paths both
believe they have the right to release the

5
00:00:12.472 --> 00:00:13.701
same underlying resource.

6
00:00:13.781 --> 00:00:15.277
The core issue is not only memory.

7
00:00:15.357 --> 00:00:16.747
It is cleanup responsibility.

8
00:00:16.827 --> 00:00:19.767
Files, sockets, native handles, lock
guards, database handles,

9
00:00:19.847 --> 00:00:22.412
temporary directories, externally
allocated buffers,

10
00:00:22.492 --> 00:00:25.325
and device resources all need one clear
authority for release.

11
00:00:25.405 --> 00:00:27.543
When responsibility is duplicated or
unclear,

12
00:00:27.623 --> 00:00:29.814
lifecycle bugs become much easier to
introduce.

13
00:00:29.894 --> 00:00:31.284
In everyday engineering work,

14
00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:35.212
double-free risk often appears where a
Rust abstraction meets a manual cleanup rule.

15
00:00:35.292 --> 00:00:36.735
A library might expose a handle,

16
00:00:36.815 --> 00:00:39.327
a platform API might require a matching
close function,

17
00:00:39.407 --> 00:00:41.331
or a wrapper might support early shutdown.

18
00:00:41.411 --> 00:00:44.404
The question is always the same: which
path owns cleanup right now?

19
00:00:44.484 --> 00:00:47.584
Rust changes the default model by
assigning each value a clear owner.

20
00:00:47.664 --> 00:00:50.978
Safe Rust ties cleanup to ownership and
runs cleanup when ownership ends.

21
00:00:51.058 --> 00:00:53.142
Moved values cannot keep acting as old
owners,

22
00:00:53.222 --> 00:00:56.376
and ordinary borrowed references do not
own cleanup responsibility.

23
00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:59.609
Those rules make duplicate cleanup
difficult to express in safe code.

24
00:00:59.689 --> 00:01:02.896
That safety story is strongest inside
Rust's normal ownership system.

25
00:01:02.976 --> 00:01:05.114
Risk can re-enter at unsafe code, raw
pointers,

26
00:01:05.194 --> 00:01:08.027
custom allocation boundaries, foreign
function interfaces,

27
00:01:08.107 --> 00:01:10.405
external cleanup functions, callback
contracts,

28
00:01:10.485 --> 00:01:14.119
and low-level wrappers where the compiler
cannot prove the ownership contract.

29
00:01:14.199 --> 00:01:17.086
The defensive goal is one clear cleanup
authority per resource.

30
00:01:17.166 --> 00:01:20.319
If a design needs shared ownership, use
coordinated ownership tools.

31
00:01:20.399 --> 00:01:23.552
If a design crosses unsafe or external
boundaries, document who owns,

32
00:01:23.632 --> 00:01:25.610
who frees, which cleanup function applies,

33
00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.668
and how error paths change responsibility.

34
00:01:27.748 --> 00:01:30.420
Every exception should be explicit,
tested, and reviewed.
